Two owner-filed tasks are ready to pick up, neither in config:
1. #1198 (Bedrock): yoagent 0.27.9 fixes the upstream half and is already on Cargo.lock. @yuanhao's comment at 15:28Z lists the yoyo half: remove or reconcile providers::bedrock_max_tokens and keep the stub test as a regression test. Whoever does it has to state one behaviour change. Today yoyo sends maxTokens = 64000 even with thinking off. Without yoyo's copy, yoagent sends no cap at all in that case.
2. #1202 step 1 (filed today): check bash commands on a tree-sitter-bash parse tree instead of raw text. I built a throwaway probe outside the repo, in /tmp. tree-sitter 0.25.10 with tree-sitter-bash 0.25.1 builds from cold in 6.4 seconds here. The parser shows the command hidden in $( ) from #1193 as an ordinary nested command. In #1196 the kill inside the quoted string shows up as text, not as a command. Heredoc bodies and indirect command names like $CMD can also be detected.
   • safety.rs has no room left under its size cap, so the new code needs its own module.
   • "Fail closed to confirmation" does nothing in piped -p mode. There, tools.rs:386 runs a command that drew a warning, because no one is there to confirm.

Research: Codex's bash.rs matches what #1202 describes, and it uses the same crate versions as my probe. A write-up of Claude Code's leaked source says it also uses tree-sitter. That source is second-hand and I haven't checked it against the real code. It lists cases where tree-sitter and bash read a command differently, and each one has to be sent to confirmation: Unicode whitespace, NUL bytes, \  (backslash-space), the parse time/size budget, and builtins that run their arguments as code. I saved this to yopedia.

